  ### 
    [Signal equality functions](https://angular.dev/guide/signals#signal-equality-functions)


  When creating a signal, you can optionally provide an equality function, which will be used to check whether the new value is actually different than the previous one.

```
import isEqual from 'lodash/isEqual';
const data = 
```
[signal](https://angular.dev/api/core/signal)(['test'], {equal: isEqual});
// Even though this is a different array instance, the deep equality
// function will consider the values to be equal, and the signal won't
// trigger any updates.
data.set(['test']);
Equality functions can be provided to both writable and computed signals.

**HELPFUL:** By default, signals use referential equality ([`Object.is()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/is) comparison).

### 
    [Type checking signals](https://angular.dev/guide/signals#type-checking-signals)


  You can use [`isSignal`](https://angular.dev/api/core/isSignal) to check if a value is a [`Signal`](https://angular.dev/api/core/Signal):

`const count =` [signal](https://angular.dev/api/core/signal)(0);
const doubled = [computed](https://angular.dev/api/core/computed)(() => count() * 2);
[isSignal](https://angular.dev/api/core/isSignal)(count); // true
[isSignal](https://angular.dev/api/core/isSignal)(doubled); // true
[isSignal](https://angular.dev/api/core/isSignal)(42); // false
To specifically check if a signal is writable, use [`isWritableSignal`](https://angular.dev/api/core/isWritableSignal):

`const count =` [signal](https://angular.dev/api/core/signal)(0);
const doubled = [computed](https://angular.dev/api/core/computed)(() => count() * 2);
[isWritableSignal](https://angular.dev/api/core/isWritableSignal)(count); // true
[isWritableSignal](https://angular.dev/api/core/isWritableSignal)(doubled); // false
